# Visual System Rules — The Art Director

Read `00-brand-core.mdc` first. The design has to carry the attitude even with the sound off —
a lot of this traffic arrives from silent autoplay clips on social.

## Visual intensity increases with scroll depth

Mirror the copy escalation rule visually: background gets darker, type gets tighter/heavier,
CTA buttons get bigger, as the visitor moves down the page. A page that looks visually flat top
to bottom is a bug, same as copy that reads flat top to bottom. Concretely, that means:

- **No floor before the last section.** The depth-background ramp (`--color-depth-0` through
  `-12` in `styles.css`) must keep decreasing in lightness every step, all the way to the last
  call. Plateauing early (multiple consecutive sections sharing one shade) shipped once already
  and read as flat — don't reintroduce a floor to "simplify" the scale.
- **One CTA carries full weight per section.** When a section shows two CTAs (e.g. Hero), only
  one is solid-gold; the other renders as the ghost/secondary variant (`link_button.tsx`'s
  `variant="ghost"`). Two equal-weight solid buttons in the same viewport is a hierarchy bug.
- **A singled-out choice needs elevation, not just a label.** If a comparison (pricing tier,
  package, plan) marks one option as recommended/featured, a star or border alone doesn't cut
  it — it needs actual visual lift (scale, shadow, or a ribbon) so it reads as raised before
  anyone reads the label.

## The nautical metaphor is load-bearing

Sea, sail, port, rocks, anchor, drift, crew, ship — this vocabulary should show up in visual
motifs (icons, dividers, section transitions), not just copy. New visual metaphors should
extend this language, not introduce an unrelated one (no generic tech/rocket/lightning-bolt
iconography).

## Scroll-position indicator

Shipped as `components/distance-meter.tsx` — a thin progress bar styled as "distance from port,"
mounted globally in `routes/index.tsx`. Don't duplicate this per-section; extend this component
if a section needs its own depth cue.

## Sticky CTA

Shipped as `components/sticky-cta.tsx`, driven by `lib/use-active-section.ts`. Label changes per
section per `02-conversion-funnel.mdc` — one button, evolving language, not a static "Apply Now."
New sections add an entry to `LABEL_BY_SECTION` in that file, not a second sticky element.

## What to avoid

- No stock photography. No generic SaaS icon packs (rocket ships, lightbulbs, handshake icons).
- No feature-grid components with neutral labels ("Compare Plans," "Feature Breakdown") — if a
  comparison table exists, its headers and framing need copy per `01-copywriting-standards.mdc`,
  not default component labels.
- No soft pastel or corporate-blue-and-white palettes that could belong to any SaaS company.
  The current navy/dark palette is correct — protect it.

## Color tokens: why `oklch()` in `styles.css`

Tokens use **oklch** (lightness, chroma, hue), not hex, because:

1. **Perceptually even steps** — the `--color-depth-0` … `--color-depth-12` scroll ramp changes
   lightness without hue drift; hex/navy tweaks shift color unpredictably.
2. **Load-bearing depth mechanic** — scroll escalation depends on 13 distinct navy steps; oklch
   makes that scale maintainable (see `design-system/07-depth-scroll-intensity.md`).
3. **Functional split** — gold (`oklch(0.80 0.14 85)`) for CTAs vs rust (`oklch(0.62 0.16 35)`)
   for wrong-state / agitation; separate hues in a uniform space, not one orange for everything.
4. **Modern stack** — Tailwind 4 + `@theme inline` expect CSS color functions; oklch is the
   current standard for design-system tokens in browser CSS.

Do not revert depth tokens to flat hex navy without an explicit human decision. See
`design-system/02-color-tokens.md` for the full token table.

